# MOF-Confined Sub-2 nm Atomically Ordered Intermetallic PdZn Nanoparticles as High-Performance Catalysts for Selective Hydrogenation of Acetylene

## abstract
Controllable synthesis of ultrasmall atomically ordered intermetallic nanoparticles is a challenging task, owing to the high temperature commonly required for the formation of intermetallic phases. Here, a metal-organic framework (MOF)-confined co-reduction strategy is developed for the preparation of sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles, by employing the well-defined porous structures of calcinated ZIF-8 (ZIF-8C) and an in situ co-reduction therein. HAADF-STEM, HRTEM, and EDS characterizations reveal the homogeneous dispersion of these sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles within the ZIF-8C frameworks. XRD, XPS, and EXAFS measurements further confirm the atomically ordered intermetallic phase nature of these sub-2 nm PdZn nanoparticles. Selective hydrogenation of acetylene evaluation results show the excellent catalytic properties of the sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn, which result from the energetically more favorable path for acetylene hydrogenation and ethylene desorption over the ultrasmall particles than over larger-sized intermetallic PdZn as revealed by density functional theory (DFT) calculations. Moreover, this protocol is also extendable for the preparation of sub-2 nm intermetallic PtZn nanoparticles and is expected to provide a novel methodology in synthesizing ultrasmall atomically ordered intermetallic nanomaterials by rationally functionalizing MOFs.

Controllable synthesis of ultrasmall atomically ordered intermetallic nanoparticles is a challenging task, owing to the high temperature commonly required for the formation of intermetallic phases. Here, a metal–organic framework (MOF)-confined co-reduction strategy is developed for the preparation of sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles, by employing the well-defined porous structures of calcinated ZIF-8 (ZIF-8C) and an in situ co-reduction therein. HAADF-STEM, HRTEM, and EDS characterizations reveal the homogeneous dispersion of these sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles within the ZIF-8C frameworks. XRD, XPS, and EXAFS measurements further confirm the atomically ordered intermetallic phase nature of these sub-2 nm PdZn nanoparticles. Selective hydrogenation of acetylene evaluation results show the excellent catalytic properties of the sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn, which result from the energetically more favorable path for acetylene hydrogenation and ethylene desorption over the ultrasmall particles than over larger-sized intermetallic PdZn as revealed by density functional theory (DFT) calculations. Moreover, this protocol is also extendable for the preparation of sub-2 nm intermetallic PtZn nanoparticles and is expected to provide a novel methodology in synthesizing ultrasmall atomically ordered intermetallic nanomaterials by rationally functionalizing MOFs.
Atomically ordered intermetallic nanoparticles have exhibited intriguing properties in a series of applications, by virtue of their synergistic geometric/electronic effect.[1–4] However, traditional methods such as the high-temperature melting only produce bulk intermetallic materials with limited surfaceexposed active sites.[5] Solution route can make well-defined intermetallic nanoparticles through a capping-ligand-assisted strategy, but the high temperature demanded in the formation of intermetallic phases generally leads to larger sized (above $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) intermetallic nanoparticles.[6,7] Besides, the undesired blocking of surface active sites by the capping ligand or serious particle aggregation after removing it generally occurs.[8] To fabricate ultrasmall intermetallic nanoparticles with high stability remains a great challenge. Here, we have successfully prepared sub- $\cdot2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn in porous ZIF-8C frameworks (PdZn-sub-2 $@$ ZIF-8C) through a metal– organic framework (MOF)-confined coreduction route that employs the confining effect of the uniform porous structures of ZIF-8C and the in situ co-reduction of Pd and $Z\mathrm{n}$ within the ZIF-8C frameworks. The uniform pores of ZIF-8C served as “cages” effectively restrict particle aggregation in preparation and endow the prepared sub- $2\:\mathrm{\nm}$ intermetallic $\operatorname{PdZn}$ with excellent size uniformity and thermal stability. Strikingly, after $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ calcination, the size distribution of these PdZn-sub- $2@Z\mathrm{IF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ is well retained without obvious sintering. The as-synthesized intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles are further employed for catalytic evaluation toward selective hydrogenation of acetylene, an industrially important reaction.[9–12] Catalytic evaluation results demonstrate excellent catalytic properties of the PdZn-sub-2 $@$ ZIF-8C as compared with their larger sized intermetallic PdZn counterparts. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations reveal that sub- $\cdot2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn is energetically more favorable for acetylene hydrogenation and ethylene desorption, which is intimately connected with the excellent catalytic activity and selectivity of it. Moreover, this protocol is also extendable to other systems, as can be demonstrated by the successful preparation of sub-2 nm intermetallic PtZn nanoparticles. It is further found that a proper crystal formation energy is of key importance in obtaining ultrasmall intermetallic compounds as disclosed by the DFT calculations, which may serve as an effective design guideline for screening and fabricating ultrasmall intermetallic nanoparticles.
The schematic preparation process of PdZn-sub-2 $@$ ZIF-8C is displayed in Figure 1a. By a MOF-confined co-reduction process within the ZIF-8C frameworks (see the Experimental Section and Figures S1 and S2 in the Supporting Information. To be noted, after calcination treatment, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) peaks of ZIF-8 have totally dispersed in ZIF-8C, PdZnsub-2 $@$ ZIF-8C was successfully prepared. Figure 1b shows the high-angle annular fark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image of the PdZn-1.2 $@$ ZIF-8C. The average particle size of it is about $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ with a narrow size distribution as displayed in the inset of Figure 1b and Figure S3 (Supporting Information). Detailed high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) examinations of different regions of the PdZn-1.2 $@$ ZIF-8C are shown in Figure S4 (Supporting Information), which reveals that the ultrasmall $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles are well embedded in the ordered pores of ZIF-8C. This effective confinement endows the as-formed particles with both uniform size and excellent thermal stability. Additionally, the uniform dispersion of these $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles within the ZIF-8C frameworks is further confirmed by the corresponding energy-dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS) elementary mapping analysis as shown in Figure 1. With a similar method, PdZn-1.8@ZIF-8C with an average particle size of $1.8~\mathrm{nm}$ and a narrow size distribution is also successfully fabricated as shown in Figure 1c and Figure S5 (Supporting Information). Larger sized $2.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn (PdZn-2.7/ ZIF-8C) and $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn (PdZn-10/ZIF-8C) (as displayed in Figures S6 and S7 (Supporting Information) and Figure 1d,f are also prepared as contrast materials. Cs-corrected HAADF-STEM image of PdZn-10/ZIF-8C in Figure 1f displays the well-defined lattice fringes, showing good crystallinity of it that is in line with following XRD characterizations. The image of PdZn-10/ZIF-8C nanoparticle viewed along [001] zone axis (as shown in Figure 1f) reveals the ordered arrangement of the bright Pd and dark Zn atom columns on {100} planes, which is in excellent agreement with the ideal crystal structure model of the intermetallic PdZn (P4/mmm) as indicated in the insets of Figure 1f. Besides, the unique fast Fourier transform (FFT) patterns and lattice fringes assigned for (200) and (020) planes further confirm the tetragonal intermetallic PdZn phase nature of the PdZn-10/ZIF-8C.[13,14] However, as for the sub- $.2\mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn, particle aggregation occurred under the strong electron beam irradiation during aberration-corrected HAADFSTEM observing. To elucidate the intermetallic phase nature of these sub- $2{\mathrm{~nm~PdZn}}$ nanoparticles, we further employ XRD, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), and the X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) characterizations that are commonly used for determination of intermetallic phase, which will be discussed in the following part. Figure 2a shows the XRD patterns of the PdZn-1.2 $@$ ZIF-8C, PdZn-1.8@ZIF-8C, $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}2.7/$ ZIF-8C, PdZn-10/ZIF-8C, and ZIF-8C. The ZIF-8C exhibits a broad peak at around $25^{\circ}$ , which is attributed to the characteristic peak of graphitic carbon.[15] As revealed in Figure 2a, XRD patterns of the PdZn-10/ZIF-8C and PdZn-2.7/ZIF-8C are in excellent agreement with the intermetallic PdZn (JCPDS 65-9523). Characteristic XRD reflection peaks of intermetallic PdZn are also found in the enlarged pictures for $\operatorname{PdZn-1}.8@$ ZIF-8C as displayed in Figure 2a. However, as for the PdZn$1.2@\mathrm{ZIF-8C}$ , corresponding XRD reflection peaks tended to disappear, due to the ultrasmall particle size as has been revealed by reported findings.[16] The intermetallic PdZn phase nature of these sub-2 nm PdZn nanoparticles was further confirmed by XPS and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) characterizations. Figure 2b is the Pd 3d XPS spectra of the prepared 1.2, 1.8, and 2.7 nm PdZn nanoparticles. Binding energy positions of these three samples were similar and centered at 335.6 and $340.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ which were ascribed to $\mathrm{Pd}_{5/2}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}_{3/2}$ peaks showing $0.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ positive shift as compared with that of metallic Pd. This result was in good accordance with previous reports for intermetallic PdZn.[17] Figure 2c and d are the Fourier transforms of the Pd K-edge EXAFS oscillations of the fabricated PdZn nanoparticles. Figure 2c reveals that the oscillation modes of $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}1.2@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ , PdZn- $\cdot1.8@$ ZIF-8C, PdZn-2.7/ZIF-8C, and PdZn-10/ZIF-8C in the k space are similar and well in line with the simulated standard peaks of intermetallic PdZn and previous reports,[18,19] demonstrating intermetallic phase nature of these PdZn nanoparticles. This result was further evidenced by the corresponding oscillation modes in the R space. As displayed in Figure 2d, the oscillation modes of the prepared PdZn nanoparticles in the R space are also in excellent agreement with the simulated standard peaks of intermetallic PdZn, which undoubtedly reveals the intermetallic PdZn phase nature of the above prepared sub-2 nm PdZn nanoparticles. Corresponding data fitting results of the EXAFS are displayed in Table S1 (Supporting Information). It was found that the first shell neighbor of Pd was Zn coordination with a Pd-Zn bond length around $2.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The $\mathrm{Pd}{\cdot}\mathrm{Zn}$ coordination numbers of the $\mathrm{PdZn-}1.2@$ ZIF-8C, PdZn- $\cdot1.8@$ ZIF-8C, PdZn-2.7/ZIF-8C, and PdZn-10/ZIF-8C were 4.2, 4.6, 5, and 6, respectively. In principle, smaller nanoparticles afford larger surface to volume ratio and lower surface coordination numbers.[20] The alteration of the $\operatorname{Pd}\mathord{-}Z\mathrm{n}$ coordination numbers in the current case was well in line with the size variation of the prepared intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles.
The formation mechanism of the ultrasmall sub- $2\mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles is further explored as follows. The $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ of ZIF-8 can not be reduced by hydrogen gas at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to form $Z\mathrm{n}$ nanoparticles as revealed by the XRD characterizations in Figure S8 (Supporting Information), due to the strong $\scriptstyle\mathrm{N-Zn}$ bond interaction of ZIF-8. It was further found that the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zn}}$ bond interaction of ZIF-8C has been significantly weakened in comparison with ZIF-8 as revealed by the binding energy changes of corresponding Zn LMM Auger electron spectra and N 1s XPS spectra characterizations in Figure S9 (Supporting Information). The effective weakening of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zn}}$ bond in ZIF-8C support provides an intriguing opportunity to reduce Pd and $Z\mathrm{n}$ together to obtain the intermetallic PdZn compounds. As displayed in Figure 1a, by an impregnation method, the Pd precursors readily enter the welldefined pores of ZIF-8C, due to the strong adsorption effect of them.[21] Taking a subsequent $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ reduction treatment at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the Pd precursors can be easily reduced due to the high redox potential of it (0.951 V). At the same time, the near $Z\mathrm{n}$ sites to the Pd sites are also reduced through the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ spillover effect as revealed by previous reports,[10] forming well-defined intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles. The uniform pores of ZIF-8C effectively limit particle aggregation in preparation and impart the prepared sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn with ultrasmall size and excellent size homogeneity.
In practical applications, particle aggregation usually occurs during reaction process especially for small nanoparticles with high surface energy.[22] Our PdZn-sub-2 $@$ ZIF-8C was found to exhibit high thermal stability due to the effective protection of the uniform and rigid pores of ZIF-8C.[23,24] As shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), after $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ calcination, the particle sizes of the $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}1.2@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ and $\operatorname{PdZn-1}.8@$ ZIF-8C are well retained without obvious aggregation. But for the PdZn-2.7/ZIF-8C, the average particle size of which is larger than that of the uniform pores of ZIF-8C $(2.5\ \mathrm{nm})$ , serious particle aggregation was observed after $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ calcination. This result indicates the significant roles of the well-defined porous structures of ZIF-8C, which provides robust “cages” that protect the sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn and endows them with excellent size uniformity and thermal stability. We also analyzed the accessibility of these sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles to the reactant gas. As displayed in Figure S11a (Supporting
Information), the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface areas of the $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}1.2@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}1.8@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ are 381 and $377{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively, showing only a slight decrease in comparison with that of the ZIF-8C support $(\bar{3}91~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . This result demonstrated that the introduction of sub- $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles to the pores of ZIF-8C did not block the pores of the ZIF-8C support. This was further evidenced by corresponding Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) pore size distribution analysis. As shown in Figure S11b (Supporting Information), the average pore sizes of the $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}1.2@$ ZIF-8C and $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}1.8@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ are both $2.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is quite similar to the average pore size of the ZIF-8C support $(2.5\ \mathrm{nm})$ , further confirming the open structures of the pores in PdZn$1.2@\mathrm{ZIF-8C}$ and $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}1.8@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ catalyst. The molecular diameters of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are 4.2 and $3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ respectively,[25] which are much less than that of the average pore size of the PdZn-sub- $2@$ ZIF-8C catalyst $(2.3~\mathrm{nm})$ , demonstrating effective accessibility of the reactants to the sub- $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PdZn catalyst within the pores. These results reveal that the ultrafine sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles can easily contact the reactant (acetylene).
In concept, conversion is not a reflection of intrinsic activity of active sites, because catalysts commonly have different metal loadings and varied amount of active sites. Specific rate is generally employed to show the intrinsic activity of active sites.
In the current case, the prepared intermetallic PdZn catalysts also possess varied Pd loadings as shown in Table S5 (Supporting Information). The specific rate of them was further calculated. As displayed in Figure 3, the specific rate of the $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}1.2@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ is $0.049\mathrm{~mol~h^{-1}~g^{-1}};$ showing a remarkable increase than that of the PdZn-1.8@ZIF-8C $(0.035\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , PdZn-2.7/ZIF-8C $(0.031\mathrm{~\mol~\h^{-1}~\ g^{-1}})$ and $\mathrm{PdZn}{\cdot}10/\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{C}$ $(0.012\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , which is in line with the above DFT calculations. Time-on-stream stability test over the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{PdZn-1.2@ZIF-8C}$ in Figure 3 further shows that both the ethylene selectivity $(80\%)$ and acetylene conversion $(70\%)$ maintain well in $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ on stream testing process, revealing excellent catalytic stability of the $\mathrm{PdZn-}1.2@$ ZIF-8C.
With a similar method, ultrasmall sub- $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PtZn nanoparticles were also prepared as displayed in Figures S17 and S18 (Supporting Information), demonstrating effective generality of this MOF-confined co-reduction strategy. But for Ru, sub-2 nm Ru nanoparticles instead of sub- $.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic RuZn were obtained as displayed in Figure S19 (Supporting Information). According to DFT calculations in Table S6 (Supporting Information), the crystal formation energy of intermetallic PdZn and PtZn are $-1.10$ and $-1.11\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, which thermodynamically benefit the formation of intermetallic PdZn and PtZn phases. However, it is thermodynamically unfavorable to obtain intermetallic RuZn phase because of the positive crystal formation energy of it $(0.4~\mathrm{eV})$ . This finding can further serve as an effective design principle for screening and preparing ultrasmall intermetallic nanoparticles.
In summary, we have fabricated atomically ordered sub-2 nm intermetallic PdZn nanoparticles through a MOF-confined co-reduction strategy. Detailed characterizations confirm the intermetallic PdZn phase, and ultrasmall size of the prepared PdZn-sub-2 $@$ ZIF-8C. High-temperature $(600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ calcination experiment further reveals the excellent thermal stability of the PdZn-sub-2 $@$ ZIF-8C, which is endowed by the rigid and ordered pores of ZIF-8C support. Selective hydrogenation of acetylene evaluation displays the excellent catalytic properties of the PdZn-sub-2 $@$ ZIF-8C, due to the energetically favorable path for acetylene hydrogenation and ethylene desorption as revealed by the DFT calculations. This strategy is also applicable to the fabrication of sub- $.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ intermetallic PtZn nanoparticles, opening up new opportunities for preparation of ultrasmall atomically ordered intermetallic nanomaterials by rational functionalization of metal-organic frameworks MOFs.
